# vercel-insights environment
#
# Copy this file to .env, fill in real values, and keep .env out of version
# control. Every value below is a placeholder and is obviously fake.
# The tool reads these from the environment; it never reads or writes a file
# itself, so load them however you like, for example:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# Each variable has a matching flag that takes precedence over it. These five
# are the only variables the tool reads.

# REQUIRED. Vercel access token. Read scope is sufficient: this tool is
# read-only, and every request it can make comes from a fixed five endpoint
# allowlist with no write path. Create one at https://vercel.com/account/tokens
# (Vercel shows the value once, so copy it immediately). Flag: --token
# Not needed for --dry-run.
#
# SCOPE MATTERS, and the failure is confusing if you get it wrong. A token
# scoped to a single project reads Web Analytics fine, because that API scopes
# by project. It CANNOT read Speed Insights: Vercel serves those from its
# observability API, which scopes by account, so a project scoped token has no
# account to resolve and every Speed Insights preset answers
# "404 Observability Data not found." Use an account or team scoped token if
# you want both halves. To check what the current token can reach:
#   npx vercel@latest metrics schema
VERCEL_TOKEN=vercel_tok_000000000000000000000000

# REQUIRED. The project to query, as a project ID or a project name. Find the ID
# in the Vercel dashboard under Project, Settings, General, field "Project ID".
# The feature you are querying must be enabled on this project, or the query
# comes back empty. They are two separate per-project switches:
#   Web Analytics:  https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart
#   Speed Insights: https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart
# Flag: --project. The Speed Insights presets also accept --all instead, which
# queries every project in the team.
VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=prj_000000000000000000000000

# OPTIONAL. Only for a project owned by a team, not by a personal account. Find
# it in the Vercel dashboard under Team Settings, General, field "Team ID".
# Leave this commented out for personal projects. Flag: --team
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=team_000000000000000000000000

# OPTIONAL. A team slug, the alternative to VERCEL_TEAM_ID. It is the team name
# in your Vercel URLs, https://vercel.com/<slug>. Set at most one of
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID and VERCEL_TEAM_SLUG: setting both is a configuration error.
# Flag: --team-slug
# OPTIONAL, and only for Speed Insights presets. The account that owns the
# project, sent as scope.ownerId. A team IS its own owner, so setting
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID covers it. For a personal project, leave this unset and the
# tool reads your account id once from the API, or set it here to skip that
# call. Find it with: curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $VERCEL_TOKEN" \
#   https://api.vercel.com/v2/user
# VERCEL_OWNER_ID=own_000000000000000000000000
#
# VERCEL_ORG_ID is read too, and is the same thing under Vercel's own name for
# it. `vercel link` writes it into .vercel/project.json, so a standard Vercel
# setup often already has it.
# VERCEL_ORG_ID=team_000000000000000000000000

# VERCEL_TEAM_SLUG=example-team

# OPTIONAL. Set to any value to disable coloured output. Colour is disabled
# automatically when stdout is not a terminal. Flag: --no-color
# NO_COLOR=1
